# 6. Important Viva Questions with Answers

## A. Basic Questions

**Q1. What is Dynamic Programming?**

Dynamic Programming is an algorithmic technique that solves problems by dividing them into smaller subproblems and storing their results to avoid repeated calculations.

**Q2. What is the 0/1 Knapsack Problem?**

It is an optimization problem in which we select items to maximize profit without exceeding the given weight capacity.

**Q3. Why is it called 0/1 Knapsack?**

Because each item can either be selected completely (1) or rejected completely (0).

**Q4. Can we select half an item in 0/1 Knapsack?**

No. Items are indivisible, so we must take the complete item or leave it.

**Q5. What is the objective of the Knapsack Problem?**

To maximize the total profit while keeping the total weight within the given capacity.

**Q6. What are the two important properties of Dynamic Programming?**

Overlapping subproblems and optimal substructure.

**Q7. What are overlapping subproblems?**

When the same smaller problems are solved repeatedly while solving a larger problem.

**Q8. What is optimal substructure?**

When an optimal solution can be constructed using optimal solutions of smaller subproblems.

**Q9. What is a DP table?**

A DP table stores the results of previously solved subproblems so that they can be reused.

**Q10. What is the difference between 0/1 Knapsack and Fractional Knapsack?**

In 0/1 Knapsack, we either take the complete item or leave it. In Fractional Knapsack, we can take a fraction of an item.

## B. Algorithm-Related Questions

**Q11. Which technique is used in this assignment?**

Dynamic Programming.

**Q12. Why do we use a 2D array?**

To store the maximum profit for different numbers of items and different weight capacities.

**Q13. Why do we initialize the first row with zero?**

Because with zero available items, the maximum profit is always zero.

**Q14. Why do we initialize the first column with zero?**

Because with zero capacity, we cannot select any item.

**Q15. What does the last cell of the DP table represent?**

It represents the maximum profit that can be obtained using all items within the given capacity.

**Q16. Why do we use the max() function?**

To compare the profit obtained by including and excluding the current item.

**Q17. How do we identify selected items?**

By comparing the current DP cell with the cell directly above it while tracing backwards.

**Q18. What happens if an item's weight exceeds the current capacity?**

We cannot select that item, so we copy the value from the previous row.

**Q19. Can Dynamic Programming solve the Knapsack Problem optimally?**

Yes. The standard 0/1 Knapsack DP algorithm finds an optimal solution.

## C. Complexity-Related Questions

**Q20. What is the time complexity of 0/1 Knapsack using DP?**

O(nW), where n is the number of items and W is the capacity.

**Q21. What is its space complexity?**

O(nW), because we store a 2D DP table.

**Q22. Can we reduce the space complexity?**

Yes. We can use a one-dimensional DP array and update it backwards, reducing auxiliary space to O(W).

**Q23. What is the difference between polynomial and exponential complexity?**

Polynomial complexity grows at a rate such as O(n²), whereas exponential complexity grows at a rate such as O(2ⁿ).

**Q24. Is O(nW) polynomial time?**

It is pseudo-polynomial time because W is a numerical capacity, not necessarily the number of bits needed to represent W.

## D. Conceptual Questions

**Q25. What are the applications of Dynamic Programming?**

Matrix Chain Multiplication, Longest Common Subsequence and Travelling Salesman Problem.

**Q26. What is the difference between Greedy and Dynamic Programming?**

Greedy makes the best immediate choice at each step, whereas Dynamic Programming stores solutions to smaller subproblems to find an optimal overall solution.

**Q27. What is Branch and Bound?**

Branch and Bound is an optimization technique that explores possible solutions and eliminates branches that cannot produce a better result than the current best solution.

**Q28. What is the difference between Dynamic Programming and Branch and Bound?**

Dynamic Programming stores and reuses subproblem results, whereas Branch and Bound uses bounds to eliminate unpromising branches of the search space.

**Q29. Which is better: Dynamic Programming or Branch and Bound?**

It depends on the problem. Dynamic Programming is effective when subproblems overlap, while Branch and Bound can be useful when bounds eliminate many possible solutions.

**Q30. Is 0/1 Knapsack an NP-Complete problem?**

Yes. The decision version of 0/1 Knapsack is NP-Complete, while its optimization version is NP-Hard.

